===FAQ===
Q: How much does it cost to start a breakdown recovery business?
A: The biggest costs are the truck, insurance and equipment. A used trailer and 4x4 is the cheapest start; a used 3.5-tonne recovery truck costs more but is far more practical. Get insurance quotes before you buy anything: for many new operators the premium is a bigger monthly cost than the truck.
Q: Can I drive a recovery truck on a car licence?
A: On a category B licence you can drive vehicles up to 3,500 kg maximum authorised mass, which covers most 3.5-tonne recovery trucks. Heavier trucks need C1 (up to 7.5 tonnes) or C. Remember that the car on the back has to fit within the truck's payload.
Q: Do I need PAS 43 to start?
A: No, it isn't a legal requirement. Many contractors and larger customers ask for it, so it can open doors to that work. Whether it's worth the cost depends on where your work comes from.
Q: What insurance does a recovery business need?
A: Typically a motor trade or recovery policy that covers the work you do, public liability and goods in transit, plus employers' liability once you have staff. Use a broker who knows recovery and make sure every activity you do is declared.
Q: Should I work for the motor clubs?
A: It can give you volume when you start, but check the rates, the distances and the payment terms carefully. Many operators find that garages, trade customers and the public pay better.
Q: What should I do with jobs I can't handle?
A: Pass them to a trusted operator who can, at a fair price, and keep the customer. A list of reliable firms and what they can lift is one of the most useful things a small recovery business can have.
